    description:
      "Mental toughness correlates with positive outcomes in sport, education and mental health, and roughly half its variance appears heritable, but the concept overlaps heavily with hardiness, conscientiousness and emotional stability, and its questionnaires have disputed dimensionality. Reviews by the construct's leading researchers call for clearer definitions; intervention evidence is a dozen mostly uncontrolled or high-bias studies. The honest read: 'toughness' describes athletes who already have specific coping and attentional skills, and those skills — not a trait label — are what can be trained.",
    action: "Decline any package sold as mental-toughness training; instead pick one measurable skill (a pre-performance routine, a pressure-training drill, or imagery practice) and run it for four weeks with a coach or sport psychologist, tracking one performance metric.",
